                "event": "Flood Watch",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Jacksonville FL",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 9 at 2:47PM EDT until October 10 at 2:00AM EDT by NWS Jacksonville FL",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of northeast and northern Florida, including the\nfollowing areas, in northeast Florida, Baker, Coastal Duval,\nCoastal Flagler, Coastal Nassau, Eastern Clay, Eastern Putnam,\nInland Flagler, Inland Nassau, Northeast Coastal St. Johns,\nNorthern Inland St. Johns, South Central Duval, Southeast Coastal\nSt. Johns, Southern Inland St. Johns, Trout River, Western Clay,\nWestern Duval, Western Putnam and Western St. Johns. In northern\nFlorida, Bradford, Central Marion, Eastern Alachua, Eastern\nHamilton, Eastern Marion, Gilchrist, Northern Columbia,\nSoutheastern Columbia, Southwestern Columbia, Suwannee, Union,\nWestern Alachua, Western Hamilton and Western Marion.\n\n* WHEN...Until 2 AM EDT Saturday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- A lifting warm front and moisture due to Hurricane Isaias passing\nwest of the area will bring waves of moderate to heavy rainfall\nacross areas of Northeast Florida into this evening. Several\ninches of rain from the last several days will make for saturated\nsoils and additional rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches with\nlocally higher amounts may lead to flash flooding. The heavy\nrainfall may combine with higher than normal tidal levels and may\nworsen flood problems in some St. Johns River locations.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
